  <h2>Structure</h2>
  <p>Flight logic never touches hardware. Drivers sit behind one interface, and everything downstream takes plain data in and returns plain data out. That's what lets 195 unit tests across 15 suites run without the vehicle, and nothing in the flight code allocates from the heap.</p>
  <p>Data flows from the sensor drivers (IMU, magnetometer, barometer, GNSS) to the state estimator, then to the cascaded controller, then to the motor mixer. A flight state machine supervises all of it: boot checks, arming, takeoff, landing and failsafes.</p>
  <p>There are two attitude filters. A complementary filter seeds Madgwick at boot and keeps running, so if Madgwick goes numerically unstable the estimator falls back to it and the state machine starts a level descent. Failsafes are recomputed every tick as the highest-priority condition currently tripped, which means escalation and recovery need no special cases.</p>
  <p>I also check my tests against broken code. When I write a new suite I remove a condition or a latch on purpose and confirm that something fails.</p>

